AB  - Estuarine Ecohydrology , E. Wolanski . Elsevier , Radarweb 29, P.O. Box 211, 1000 AE Amsterdam , The Netherlands . 2007 . 168 pages . ISBN 978-0-444-53066-0 . Ecohydrology is a new interdisciplinary area that links hydrology with ecological processes. With the use of mathematical models that simulate actual ecosystems, the purpose of ecohydrology is to provide solutions for water quality and quantity problems that are now adversely affecting many coastal communities worldwide. This book tries to encourage scientists and engineers to combine their knowledge and skills so as to manage estuarine waters effectively. This is one of the few books on this new topic available today that specifically deals with estuarine ecohydrology. Estuaries, where freshwater and coastal ecosystems meet, are a complex system. The author has shown that many of these delicate estuarine ecosystems in various localities are being damaged by human activities. To restore them, they must first be understood. The first few chapters of this book focus on the components of an estuary. In Chapter 1 (Introduction), the author provides several definitions of an estuary. Then, he describes how human activities impact
