TY  - THES
TI  - Sedimentologic and Reservoir Character of a Middle-to-Late Miocene Tropical Heterozoan-Large Benthic Foraminifera-Volcaniclastic Ramp System, Los Haitises Area, Northeastern Dominican Republic
AU  - Elson Emanuel Core-Suarez
PY  - 2023
JO  - KU ScholarWorks (The University of Kansas)
UR  - http://dissertations.umi.com/ku:19254
LA  - en
AB  - This thesis documents outcrops of a middle-to-late Miocene heterozoan-photozoan transitional carbonate ramp system in the northeastern Dominican Republic that developed when the Pacific Ocean and Caribbean Sea were connected through the Central America Seaway. The system is a grainy ramp system consisting of mixed heterozoans, large benthic foraminifera, and volcaniclastic facies with photozoan corals entirely absent. Regional upwelling, sea-level fluctuation, and paleotopography were important factors controlling facies distributions and sequence architecture.Five 3rd- to 4th-order sequences developed during cycles of sea-level rises and falls. Sequences show a pattern of draping, with local thickening into lows, lap out against sloping substrate, and distinctive deepening-upward facies pattern. Basal carbonate facies overlying the sequence boundary are well sorted, have fine-grained and abraded skeletal grains, with coarse-grained volcaniclastics, and are interpreted to have been deposited in higher energy, shallow water conditions above wave base during transgressions. Basal facies grade upward to carbonate facies that are poorly sorted, have coarse-grained and unabraded skeleta
