TY  - JOUR
TI  - Networks
AU  - Jonathan Shepard
PY  - 2018
JO  - Past & Present
DO  - 10.1093/pastj/gty033
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1093/pastj/gty033
LA  - en
AB  - The essence of networks involving humans is that they are voluntary. This is so even though they can be subsumed for a while within coercive structures. Networks need not be self-declared or even self-aware, and do not necessarily entail direct contact between their human components, even in the case of the more active members of a network. They represent an aggregate of countless individuals’ appetites, orientations and conscious preferences but need not have — or wish for — a collective identity. Degrees of personal involvement in a network range from direct, organizational and continuous to fitful, tenuous and passive, while membership of multiple networks is entirely feasible. And, if full participation is optional, enforcement of generally acceptable behavioural norms and also, in commercial transactions, assurance of standards of quality may be difficult, raising the question of appropriate sanctions for glaring breaches. This catalogue of the characteristics of networks involving humans is far from comprehensive, and is in all senses contestable. But for all the problems of definition that beset ‘network’, and the glaring mismatches between theory, data and application, ‘net
