JO  - ANNALES DE L INSTITUT OCEANOGRAPHIQUE
LA  - French
AB  - Kabara is a raised limestone island covering 31 km2. About 6/7 of the island is surrounded by a barrier reef of 98% dead coral which delimits a shallow lagoon where loose sediments predominate. This lagoon has no algal areas or seagrass fields, but is crossed by 11 transverse lagoon tongues which could be remnants of older coral layers. The remaining 1/7 of the island, on the northwest side, corresponds to a collapsed zone where the ocean hits right against the cliffs fringed by the narrow apron reefs. The coral fauna, dominated by the genus Acropora both inside and outside the barrier zone, at present occupies only a small surface of the whole reef formation. There is a wide specific richness of Mollusks due to the transverse lagoon tongues that break up the monotony of the sandy lagoon; the malacological population densities are low. Fish are not numerous: the Pomacentridae are concentrated close to the lagoon tongues, but the prevalence of sandy bottoms (80% of the substratum) makes the fish fauna, which is mostly composed of Mullidae and Mugilidae, very sparse. The planktonic vegetable biomass was discussed. Apparently, Kabara is presently being, or has been, raised and this ha
