AB  - The coral reef at Dengloujiao (20°13′N～20°17′N, 109°54′E～20°58′E), Leizhou Peninsula, northern coast of the South China Sea, is the only developed and well preserved coral fringing reef on mainland China. It is part of the so-called “coral triangle” of the Southeast Asia (Robert et al, 2002, Science), where numerous important coral species are supposed to be found. The reef flat, dominated by Goniopora and Porites corals, is about 10 km long and 500～1000 m wide (2 km maximum width). From sea to land, six ecological zones can be recognized. They are (1) reef-front living coral zone (10～120 m wide) with dominant Acropora corals growing below the low spring tide; (2) outer reef-flat massive Porites zone (100～150 m wide) dominated by large dead Porites corals; (3) within-reef-flat mixed massive coral zone (~200 m wide) consisting of large dead Porites, Pavona and Favia corals; (4) within-reef-flat mixed dead massive-coral /Goniopora zone (0～120 m wide) with smaller massive corals mixed with branchy Goniopora corals; and (5) inner reef-flat Goniopora zone (100～500 m wide) dominated by dead branchy Goniopora corals with a spatial coverage of 95%. Over these zones, total 13 families, 25 g
