AB  - The ninth volume of the Annual Review of Marine Science brings its readership outstanding and fascinating reviews from leading experts across the numerous subdisciplines of marine science. In each volume, we strive to have a mix of focused disciplinary reviews together with reviews that analyze broader interdisciplinary topics. We endeavor to introduce each volume with an autobiographical piece that presents a personal perspective from a leader in marine science. This year, physical oceanographer Paola MalanotteRizzoli describes the paths and influences that shaped her career. She highlights how one geographical location, Venice, presented scientific challenges that were an enduring inspiration. The journal continues to be an outlet for reviews on the effects of climate change on ocean processes and ecosystems. This volume includes an article on paleoceanographic evidence linking the Atlantic meridional overturning circulation to abrupt climate change over the last glacial cycle, as well as articles that describe the role of climate variability in anchovy and sardine fisheries and the impacts of stressors on coral reef systems. In ocean physics, this volume presents a comprehensive
