AB  - On a global scale, coral bleaching and ocean acidification resulting from climate change have become the main threats to coral reefs. On regional to local scales where humans can have a more direct environmental control, terrestrial pollution, especially heavy sedimentation, has become the main threat to inshore corals. Here, monthly hydrologic simulations focusing on sedimentation from 2004 to 2022 using the Soil and Water Assessment Tool was done for eleven selected watersheds around the Verde Island Passage (VIP), a marine corridor declared as the world’s center of the center of marine shore fish biodiversity. Reasonable agreement between model outputs and observations (e.g., DPWH river gauges, news articles) suggests reliability of the SWAT model. Model results indicate that relative maxima of sedimentation coincide with precipitation maxima and consecutive rainfall events. Dependence of sedimentation on slope class and land use was also observed, identifying Mindoro island in the southern side of the VIP as a critical source of land surface erosion. To gain insights on the effects of Land Use Land Cover (LULC) change, LULC maps from 2017 and 2022 were used in separate simulati
