TY  - CPAPER
TI  - An Adaptive Fuzzy Logic-Based Three-Layer Model for Predicting Invasive Species Across Gulf of Mannar Zones
AU  - S. Divya
AU  - M Sumithra
AU  - V. Deepa
AU  - Subha Rathi Priya
PY  - 2025
JO  - International Conference on Computing, Communication and Automation
DO  - 10.1109/iccca66364.2025.11325570
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1109/iccca66364.2025.11325570
AB  - In the Gulf of Mannar, particularly across the 10 islands of the Keelakarai and Vembar groups, the presence of the non-native species Carijoa riisei, commonly known as snowflake coral, has been increasingly observed. Coral reefs, which form the foundation of this rich ecosystem, support the survival and proliferation of various native marine species. However, the invasion of snowflake coral poses a serious threat by smothering these reefs and inhibiting their ability to absorb oxygen, thereby disrupting the ecological balance and endangering other vital marine life. Although biological control methods for eradicating Carijoa riisei may be cost-effective, the potential environmental impacts of introducing bio-control agents must be carefully evaluated. To address this issue, a three-layer adaptive fuzzy logic-based prediction model has been developed to assess the likelihood of non-native species’ presence across different zones in the Gulf of Mannar. The model incorporates three key zones and applies fuzzy inference rules to estimate which zone is most likely to harbor snowflake corals. According to the prediction, Zone C has the highest concentration of non-native species at 7.98%
